,5733,1506,553,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,488,259,24984,220,18,83,24984,220,16,481,259,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,4622,4621,311,279,11031,323,264,1459,389,279,11031,382,5338,11,10542,264,1459,389,279,1555,13,3197,17767,83,284,220,15,59,701,279,1459,374,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,25547,15,8,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,15,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,568,2055,11,279,1459,1124,1188,16,11,220,15,11,220,16,10699,8,15448,389,279,11031,382,5847,11,1477,279,5106,4621,315,279,1555,13,576,5106,4621,374,2661,553,279,36829,315,17767,83,57758,304,279,5733,1506,23606,11,892,374,1124,11520,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,18,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,3593,785,4622,4621,311,279,11031,646,387,1730,553,4633,279,5312,1985,315,279,5106,4621,315,279,1555,323,894,4621,429,15448,304,279,11031,13,1205,646,990,279,4621,504,279,1459,1124,1188,16,11,220,15,11,220,16,10699,8,311,2441,1459,389,279,1555,11,1977,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,25547,16,8,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,568,576,4621,374,1124,11520,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,481,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,15,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,18,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe line parameterized by\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\]lies in a plane of the form $x + y + cz = d.$  Enter the ordered pair $(c,d).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"92084eefdc3748944f8cc477c0a167c99685897b5547645efea656ebe186152b"},"seconds":39.11322331428528,"seed":144858483316681177,"stop":"eos","text":"Since the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane. We can choose the vector from the point \\((1,0,1)\\) to another point on the line, say for \\(t=1\\), which gives the point \\((2,3,0)\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTo find the normal vector to the plane, we need another independent vector in the plane. Let's use the parameter \\(t = -1\\), which gives the point \\((0, -3, 2)\\). The vector from \\((1,0,1)\\) to \\((0, -3, 2)\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNow, we find the cross product of the two vectors in the plane:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & 3 & -1 \\\\\n-1 & -3 & 1\n\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(3 \\cdot 1 - (-1)(-3)) - \\mathbf{j}(1 \\cdot 1 - (-1)(-1)) + \\mathbf{k}(1(-3) - (-1)(3))\n= \\mathbf{i}(3 - 3) - \\mathbf{j}(1 - 1) + \\mathbf{k}(-3 + 3)\n= \\mathbf{i}(0) - \\mathbf{j}(0) + \\mathbf{k}(0).\n\\]\n\nThis approach shows that the vectors are linearly dependent, indicating we might need a different approach. Instead, let's directly substitute the points into the plane equation:\nFor \\((1,0,1)\\):\n\\[\n1 + 0 + c \\cdot 1 = d \\implies 1 + c = d.\n\\]\nFor \\((2,3,0)\\):\n\\[\n2 + 3 + c \\cdot 0 = d \\implies 5 = d.\n\\]\nThus, \\(1 + c = 5 \\implies c = 4\\).\n\nThe ordered pair \\((c,d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(4, 5)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(4, 5)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Since the direction vector of the line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need another vector in the plane. We can choose the vector from the point \\((1,0,1)\\) to another point on the line, say for \\(t=1\\), which gives the point \\((2,3,0)\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTo find the normal vector to the plane, we need another independent vector in the plane. Let's use the parameter \\(t = -1\\), which gives the point \\((0, -3, 2)\\). The vector from \\((1,0,1)\\) to \\((0, -3, 2)\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNow, we find the cross product of the two vectors in the plane:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & 3 & -1 \\\\\n-1 & -3 & 1\n\\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(3 \\cdot 1 - (-1)(-3)) - \\mathbf{j}(1 \\cdot 1 - (-1)(-1)) + \\mathbf{k}(1(-3) - (-1)(3))\n= \\mathbf{i}(3 - 3) - \\mathbf{j}(1 - 1) + \\mathbf{k}(-3 + 3)\n= \\mathbf{i}(0) - \\mathbf{j}(0) + \\mathbf{k}(0).\n\\]\n\nThis approach shows that the vectors are linearly dependent, indicating we might need a different approach. Instead, let's directly substitute the points into the plane equation:\nFor \\((1,0,1)\\):\n\\[\n1 + 0 + c \\cdot 1 = d \\implies 1 + c = d.\n\\]\nFor \\((2,3,0)\\):\n\\[\n2 + 3 + c \\cdot 0 = d \\implies 5 = d.\n\\]\nThus, \\(1 + c = 5 \\implies c = 4\\).\n\nThe ordered pair \\((c,d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(4, 5)}\\).","id":"precalculus:264"}
